WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas

00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:04.320
we take a look at App
Engine Scaling Settings

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.036
to optimize our instance count.

00:00:06.036 --> 00:00:07.410
Will we find the
right trade-off?

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:08.368
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:08.368 --> 00:00:09.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.720
In a previous episode, we helped
the developer of Partly Cloudy

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.670
Image Suite reduce
her instance count

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:16.230
with some clever
shifting of workload

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.790
from the server to the client.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:20.700
While this brilliant solution
cut the number of active App

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.957
Engine instances
significantly, some folks

00:00:22.957 --> 00:00:24.540
have pointed out
that we really should

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.390
have adjusted the scaling
values of the App Engine YAML

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.650
file instead.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.572
Now in complete honesty,
I've never actually messed

00:00:31.572 --> 00:00:33.030
with any of the
settings in detail,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.730
so I had no idea what
they did, why they did it,

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.630
or why I should care about that.

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:39.930
But that's what we do here
on Cloud Performance Atlas--

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.250
we figure this stuff out.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.822
As such, it was time to dig
into the Scaling Setting file

00:00:43.822 --> 00:00:45.780
and figure out how they
influenced the instance

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:46.890
count of our text.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.977
Now I made some nice
charts and things,

00:00:48.977 --> 00:00:51.060
but before we get to that,
I have to take a moment

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.920
and talk about how
App Engine decides

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.042
to start a new instance.

00:00:54.042 --> 00:00:56.250
I mean, we can't really
start tweaking these settings

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:57.820
unless we know what
we're touching.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.150
See, the App Engine's serving
algorithms are constantly

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.400
deciding on whether it's
better to queue up a request

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.747
or spin up a new instance.

00:01:03.747 --> 00:01:05.580
This takes into account
a significant number

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.980
of factors for this
decision, like request

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.800
queue depth, average queries
per second, average request

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.870
latency, et cetera,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.850
But to make things
more simple, let's

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.650
focus on the four
primary systems

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.200
that you can consider in your
configuration file, which

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.230
can directly impact the
decisions that App Engine makes

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:23.880
about spinning up new instances.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.070
Number one is the number
of concurrent requests

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:26.880
per instance.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.030
Number two, how long a request,
can sit in the work queue.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.760
Number three, if there's any
resident instances available.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.940
And number four, what type
of instances you're using.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.980
In order to understand
how all of these things

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.620
played together, I decided
to run a little stress test

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.840
to see how many instances
we could spin up

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.850
on the default setting with
four seconds of static response

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:46.350
time.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:49.880
Now, obviously this isn't
a real-world scenario.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.810
Now, when we throw 100
threads of connections

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.650
at our application
for about 10 minutes,

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:57.010
we end up with a beautiful graph
like the Google Cloud Console

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:58.660
has that looks like this.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.380
My completely
unrealistic scenario

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:02.350
topped out at
about 100 instances

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.882
to handle my work-- the
blue line right there.

00:02:04.882 --> 00:02:07.090
So we can start tweaking
some of the scaling settings

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:08.931
and see how that value responds.

00:02:08.931 --> 00:02:10.180
Let's start with an easy one--

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:11.590
concurrent requests.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:14.306
In order to optimize performance
versus instance count,

00:02:14.306 --> 00:02:16.180
you can control the max
number of connections

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:18.070
you have per instance.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.020
The Max Concurrent
Request setting

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.670
allows you to
control this value.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:25.000
The default for this is 8, which
we saw a graph for already.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.384
I changed to 80 and
re-ran the test.

00:02:27.384 --> 00:02:29.050
You can see that the
number of instances

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.140
dropped significantly from
100 to around 20 or so,

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:33.730
which is important
since this means

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.640
my bill has been cut by a
significant amount as well.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.630
The trade here is
straightforward.

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:40.180
The higher the value,
the less number

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.580
of instances, you need
to spin up connections.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.529
However, if you set it
too high, then you're

00:02:44.529 --> 00:02:46.570
going to see latency
problems as your instance is

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:47.710
strapped for resources.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:50.530
Or it may cause new
instances to be spun up

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:53.710
if you eclipse the available
memory of your machine.

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:55.330
Lowering this value
can help you find

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:57.329
the sweet spot between
the number of connections

00:02:57.329 --> 00:02:59.050
per instance, the
resources needed

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:00.610
for your request,
and the resources

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:02.870
for your particular
instance class.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:04.390
So let's reset that
back to default

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:07.120
and see what the Pending
Latency settings do.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.940
See, when a request comes
in, App Engine's front ends

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:12.220
will place it in a queue
until an instance becomes

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:14.030
available to
service the request.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:16.270
If there are no available
instances waiting around,

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:17.915
then App Engine has
to make a decision.

00:03:17.915 --> 00:03:20.290
Either it waits for one of
the instances become available

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.210
or it starts up a new instance.

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.220
The values of min/max
pending latency

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:27.130
directly control how the
scheduler will allow a request

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:30.490
to wait in this queue before
spinning up new instances.

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:33.760
The higher the min
pending latency value,

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.130
the longer a request
will wait in the queue

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.380
before triggering a new
instance to be spawned.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:41.350
Changing this from our default
value of 30 milliseconds

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:43.930
to 6 seconds, we can see
that the number of instances

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:46.300
generally drop compared
to base use case.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:49.150
It looks like we're topping
out at about 65 instances

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:50.530
or so for this test.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.940
Now max pending latency,
on the other hand,

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:55.000
is the time after
which App Engine

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.550
must start a new
instance, if a request

00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:59.170
has been waiting this long.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:01.480
Setting this value to 4
seconds doesn't really

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:03.760
see much change in the
total number of instances

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.410
that were spawning,
but it might just

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:08.020
be due to the constant load
that's being put on the system.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:11.020
I mean it'll probably change
once the workloads become

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:13.120
a little bit more variable.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.860
Now, all of these
settings so far

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:16.660
have dealt with the
requests themselves,

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:18.993
but we haven't really talked
about giving more resources

00:04:18.993 --> 00:04:20.050
to the incidents.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:22.660
It should be no surprise to you
that your App Engine instances

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:24.850
are running on some
physical piece of hardware

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:27.820
somewhere in the world,
and the slower the machine,

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:30.010
the longer it takes
to do heavy workloads,

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:32.990
leaving requests in the
request queue for longer.

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:35.620
The trick here is to request
the least powerful machine

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:37.810
possible that
handles your workload

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:41.290
and doesn't force new instances
to be created as a result.

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:44.320
The default for the instance
class variable is F1.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:47.170
So let's test an F4
instance, and see

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:49.327
how that changes things up.

00:04:49.327 --> 00:04:51.160
We can see that for our
particular workload,

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.400
changing this from F1
to F4 didn't really

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.500
do much to the overall
number of instances.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.360
However, I'd expect that to
change if we were actually

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.630
CPU- or memory-bound instead
of just sleeping for 4 seconds.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:03.666
OK, OK, OK.

00:05:03.666 --> 00:05:05.290
So we've tweaked to
each setting and we

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:07.000
have a clear picture
of what it does

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.900
and how it impacts
our instance count.

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:10.570
So let's find the sweet spot.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:13.000
This means that for our
4-second block of work,

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.310
we want the average
latency for a response

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:17.500
to be as close to 4
seconds as possible.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:20.080
To do this, let's set
Instance Class to F4,

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:23.470
Max Concurrent Requests
to 80, Max Pending Latency

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:26.870
to 8 seconds, and Min
Pending Latency to 6 seconds.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.890
The result gives us about 18
instances through our test

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:33.040
and only about 400 milliseconds'
latency being added

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:34.937
to each request on average.

00:05:34.937 --> 00:05:37.270
Now from here we can start
tweaking the settings to make

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.020
trade-offs between the
number of instances

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:41.890
we're willing to pay for and
the latency overhead it causes

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:45.310
for our users, which is exactly
the spot that we want to be in.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.950
However, this won't solve all
of your performance problems

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:48.502
in the Cloud.

00:05:48.502 --> 00:05:50.710
That's why you should check
out the rest of the Cloud

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:52.270
Performance Atlas videos.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:54.550
And remember, that when
it comes to your users,

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:56.080
every millisecond counts.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:58.530
[MUSIC PLAYING]

